## Select an organization

Organization-scoped commands resolve the organization in this order:

1. The global `--org <organization-id>` flag, for that invocation only.
2. A non-empty `PHOTON_ORGANIZATION_ID` environment variable.
3. The organization of the project in the folder's
   [`.photon/project.json`](/cli/projects#select-an-active-project).
4. The organization you stored with `photon org use`.
5. The organization your account belongs to, when it belongs to exactly one.

With several organizations and none of the first four, commands fail with
`ORGANIZATION_NOT_SELECTED` and exit `2`; run `photon org list`, then
`photon org use <organization-id>`.

```sh
photon org show                                                  # the organization and where it came from
photon org use pho_org_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s                # store an active organization
photon org member list --org pho_org_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2t  # one invocation only
photon org clear                                                 # forget the stored selection
```

Before a command changes anything in an organization you didn't name with
`--org`, `project create` included, it names the organization and where it
came from on stderr, for example
`Organization: pho_org_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s, from your only organization` or
`Organization: pho_org_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s, from /srv/support-bot/.photon/project.json`.
A command that knows its organization before it asks for confirmation prints
the line before the question, `--org` included.

`org show` reports the source as `flag`, `environment`, `folder` (with the
file's `path`), `stored`, or `default`. `org use` reads the organization
before storing it, so you can never make an inaccessible ID active. With
`--json`, both print the organization as the API returns it under
`organization`, beside `source`. `org use` and `org clear` warn you when
`PHOTON_ORGANIZATION_ID` or the folder's project still selects another
organization.

`org list` marks the active organization and the ones you own. With `--json`,
it prints the API's response, where `ownedOrganizationIds` lists the ones you
own, and adds the active organization's ID as `activeOrganizationId`. When no
organization can be selected, nothing is marked active and
`activeOrganizationId` is left out:

```json
{"acquisitionQuestionnaireExempt":false,"organizations":[{"avatarUrl":null,"billingInitialization":"completed","createdAt":"2026-07-01T00:00:00.000Z","name":"Acme","organizationId":"pho_org_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s","updatedAt":"2026-07-02T00:00:00.000Z","version":"3"}],"ownedOrganizationIds":["pho_org_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s"],"activeOrganizationId":"pho_org_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s"}
```

The stored organization is tied to the authenticated identity and API origin.
If it becomes unreadable, `org list` still works, and `org use` or `org clear`
replaces it.

These commands use the selected organization:

- `photon project list`, `project count`, and `project create`
- `photon org member`, `org invitation`, `org leave`, `org oauth-client`, and
  `org billing`
- `photon account 10dlc`

Commands that act on one project, such as `project view` or
`project billing plan purchase`, use the project's own organization.

## Members

```sh
photon org member list
photon org member invite --email ada@example.com --role member
photon org member edit pho_acc_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s --role admin
photon org member remove pho_acc_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s --yes
```

Invitations grant `member` or `viewer`. `member edit` can set `admin`,
`member`, or `viewer`; ownership is not changed through these commands.

Membership changes are guarded by the member's current version. The CLI reads
it before `edit`, `remove`, and `leave`, and records it with the operation, so
a [replay](/cli/operations) sends the same request. If the membership changed
in between, Photon rejects the request rather than overwriting the change.

## Invitations

`member invite` sends an invitation. Track and withdraw outstanding invitations
from the `invitation` namespace:

```sh
photon org invitation list
photon org invitation revoke pho_oinv_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s --yes
```

### Accept an invitation

Preview and acceptance **never** accept a token as a command-line argument.
Read it from a file, or pipe it through stdin with `--token-file -`:

```sh
printf '%s\n' "$PHOTON_INVITATION_TOKEN" |
  photon org invitation preview --token-file - --json

photon org invitation accept --token-file ./invitation-token.txt
```

```json
{"allProjects":true,"organizationId":"pho_org_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s","organizationName":"Acme","role":"member","status":"accepted"}
```

Without `--token-file`, the command fails and names the option; it doesn't
read piped stdin unless you pass `-`. Token values are trimmed, bounded, and
kept out of output and errors. Acceptance does not need a selected
organization: the token names it.

<Note>
  If acceptance is left unresolved, its journal record keeps the token so a
  replay does not have to ask you for it again. See
  [Recoverable operations](/cli/operations).
</Note>

## Leave an organization

`org leave` removes your own membership from the selected organization:

```sh
photon org leave --org pho_org_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s --yes --json
```

```json
{"accountId":"pho_acc_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s","createdAt":"2026-08-01T18:00:00.000Z","joinedVia":"invitation","organizationId":"pho_org_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s","removedAt":"2026-08-07T18:00:00.000Z","role":"member","roleManagedAt":null,"status":"removed","updatedAt":"2026-08-07T18:00:00.000Z","version":"5"}
```

With `--json`, it prints your membership as the API returns it after the
change, with `status` `removed`. Leaving clears the organization if it was
your stored selection.

## Moved commands

These commands moved here from `photon project` and `photon account`. For one
release the old names only print the replacement and exit with code `2`; they
do not run it. The replacement keeps the global options you gave (`--org`,
`--project`, `--json`, `--debug`, `--no-color`, `--no-input`), so it acts on
the same organization and project, and leaves out the command's own options,
so it never carries a `--yes`.

| Old command | New command |
| --- | --- |
| `photon project member ...` | `photon org member ...` |
| `photon project invitation ...` | `photon org invitation ...` |
| `photon project leave` | `photon org leave` |
| `photon account oauth client ...` | `photon org oauth-client ...` |
| `photon account oauth secret ...` | `photon org oauth-client secret ...` |
| `photon account billing payment ...` | `photon org billing payment-method ...` |

```json
{"error":{"code":"COMMAND_MOVED","message":"\"photon project member\" moved to \"photon org member\".","retryable":false,"docsUrl":"https://photon.codes/docs/cli/output#errors","next":[{"description":"Use the new command","run":"photon org member list --json"}]}}
```
